* History: More pixels per second

- 1990s: 3D games want as many triangles and pixels per second as possible
- A single-core CPU computes geometry and lighting, one after another. Multi-core CPUs only came around 2005.

*Problem:* *the* *CPU* *is* *too* *slow:* low resolution, few details

- 1996: 3dfx Voodoo, a 3D add-on card: it draws the pixels of the triangles. The CPU still computes the geometry.
- 1999: NVIDIA GeForce 256, marketed as the "world's first GPU"
- Transform and lighting in hardware. Now the CPU is relieved of the geometry, too.

⮕ special parallel hardware

: Erste Dual-Core-CPUs für Desktop-PCs 2005 (AMD Athlon 64 X2, Intel Pentium D). Hyper-Threading (zwei Threads auf einem Kern) ab 2002 beim Pentium 4.
: Voodoo: reine 3D-Zusatzkarte, die neben der normalen 2D-Grafikkarte steckte und über ein Durchschleifkabel angeschlossen war. Erste Karten Ende 1996.
: Quelle: Wikipedia "3dfx": https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3dfx
: Quelle: Wikipedia "GeForce 256": https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GeForce_256

* History: Developers want their own effects

- The first GPUs had a fixed pipeline: only the effects built into the hardware
- 2001: GeForce 3 and DirectX 8: programmable vertex and pixel shaders
- For the first time, small programs run on the graphics card. For every pixel.

*Problem:* *fixed* *effects* ⮕ programmable shaders

: Quelle: Wikipedia "GeForce 3 series": https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GeForce_3_series
: Quelle: IEEE Computer Society, "The Evolution of Shaders"

* History: Compute instead of draw

- Researchers notice: the GPU is a massively parallel computer
- But you can only program it with graphics APIs: data as textures, computations as pixel shaders
- 2004: BrookGPU (Stanford) hides the graphics APIs behind C extensions
- 2006/2007: NVIDIA CUDA: programming the GPU in C, without any graphics API

*Problem:* *computing* *disguised* *as* *drawing* ⮕ CUDA

: NVIDIA stellte 2004 den Brook-Entwickler Ian Buck ein. Aus Brook wurde CUDA.
: CUDA angekündigt im November 2006 mit der G80-Architektur, Version 1.0 im Juni 2007.
: Quelle: Wikipedia "BrookGPU": https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BrookGPU
: Quelle: TechSpot, "History of the Modern Graphics Processor, Part 4": https://www.techspot.com/article/659-history-of-the-gpu-part-4/

* History: One open standard, and everybody does their own thing

2008: *OpenCL*, an open standard for GPU computing (Khronos Group), initiated by Apple.
But every vendor builds its own platform:

- *NVIDIA:* CUDA (2007)
- *AMD:* ROCm and HIP (2016)
- *Intel:* oneAPI and SYCL
- *Apple:* Metal. In 2018, Apple deprecated its own OpenCL.

NVIDIA invested continuously: libraries (cuBLAS, cuDNN), tools, documentation. PyTorch and TensorFlow build on them.

*Problem:* *fragmentation* ⮕ the de facto standard is proprietary: CUDA

: Persönliche Einschätzung: Eigentlich hat nur NVIDIA das Thema über Jahre wirklich ernst genommen. Deshalb ist der Vorsprung heute so groß.
: HIP ist bewusst fast identisch zu CUDA, damit man CUDA-Code portieren kann.
: Quelle: Khronos, "The Khronos Group Releases OpenCL 1.0 Specification" (9.12.2008): https://www.khronos.org/news/press/the_khronos_group_releases_opencl_1.0_specification
: Quelle: AppleInsider, OpenGL und OpenCL in macOS 10.14 Mojave deprecated (2018): https://forums.appleinsider.com/discussion/205791/opengl-opencl-deprecated-in-favor-of-metal-2-in-macos-10-14-mojave
: Quelle: Wikipedia "ROCm": https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ROCm
: Quelle: Wikipedia "oneAPI (compute acceleration)": https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OneAPI_(compute_acceleration)

* History: A niche for a long time

A conference around 2012: How can we speed up physics simulations with GPUs?

- GPU programming is really hard
- The work was often done by people without experience in it, e.g. students
- The hardware changed all the time. With every new generation, you started again from scratch.

For a long time, computing on the GPU outside of graphics was a niche, almost a curiosity: some image processing, rendering, simulations.

*Problem:* *hard* *to* *program,* *no* *stable* *platform* ⮕ a niche, until AI came

: Persönliche Story erzählen: Konferenz um 2012, Thema Beschleunigung physikalischer Simulationen auf der GPU.
: Überleitung: Erst mit Deep Learning wird aus der Nische der Massenmarkt.
: Quelle: eigene Erfahrung des Dozenten (Konferenz um 2012)

* History: Neural networks are matrix multiplications

- 2012: AlexNet wins the ImageNet challenge: 15.3 % top-5 error, the runner-up 26.2 %
- Trained for 5 to 6 days on two NVIDIA GTX 580 gaming cards
- Neural networks consist mostly of matrix multiplications. A perfect job for GPUs.

*Problem:* *training* *takes* *too* *long* ⮕ GPUs for AI

: Das Netz passte nicht in den Speicher einer GPU (3 GB) und wurde auf zwei GPUs aufgeteilt.
: Quelle: Wikipedia "AlexNet": https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AlexNet

* History: GPUs grow up

In the beginning, the GPU belonged to exactly one program.
If a game crashed, the graphics card was in an undefined state. Sometimes you had to restart the computer.

- *2007,* *Windows* *Vista* *(WDDM):* the operating system schedules several programs on the GPU and manages the video memory. A hanging GPU is reset after 2 seconds, without a restart.
- *2010,* *NVIDIA* *Fermi:* one unified address space, ECC memory, IEEE floating point. The first GPU really built for computing.
- *2020,* *NVIDIA* *A100* *(MIG):* one GPU split into up to 7 isolated GPUs, each with its own memory. A crash in one instance does not affect the others.

*Problem:* *one* *GPU,* *one* *program,* *no* *isolation* ⮕ memory protection and sharing ⮕ GPUs in the cloud

: Persönliche Erfahrung erzählen: Spiel abgestürzt, Grafikkarte hängt, Neustart.
: Parallele zur CPU: Speicherschutz und mehrere Prozesse gab es auf der CPU schon Jahrzehnte früher. Die GPU musste das erst lernen.
: TDR = Timeout Detection and Recovery: Ein Befehlspuffer, der länger als zwei Sekunden läuft, gilt als hängend. Windows beendet alle Grafik-Kontexte, setzt den Chip zurück und lädt den Treiber neu.
: Quelle: OpenGL.org, "OpenGL and Windows Vista": https://www.opengl.org/pipeline/article/vol003_7/
: Quelle: NVIDIA Fermi Whitepaper: https://www.nvidia.com/content/pdf/fermi_white_papers/nvidia_fermi_compute_architecture_whitepaper.pdf
: Quelle: NVIDIA Blog, "What Is a Multi-Instance GPU?": https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/multi-instance-gpus/

* History: The clock speed wall

- Until about 2005, CPUs became faster mainly by a higher clock speed
- Then Dennard scaling ended: chips became too hot (power wall)
- Since then, clock speeds have stagnated at about 4 to 6 GHz
- More performance only through more cores: parallelism instead of speed

*Problem:* *the* *clock* *speed* *does* *not* *grow* *anymore* ⮕ parallelism is the only way

: Bezug zur Vorlesung Concurrent Programming (Moore's Law).
: Beispiel: Intel stoppte 2004 ein 4-GHz-Projekt wegen der Leistungsdichte.
: Quelle: Wikipedia "Dennard scaling": https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dennard_scaling

* History: Today

- July 2025: NVIDIA is worth 4 trillion US dollars, October 2025: 5 trillion
- The most valuable company in the world
- A company that started with graphics cards for games

: Quelle: CNBC, 2025-07-09: https://www.cnbc.com/2025/07/09/nvidia-4-trillion.html
: Quelle: CNN, 2025-10-29: https://www.cnn.com/2025/10/29/tech/nvidia-5-trillion-valuation-ai

* The GPU programming model: a Parallel For

A normal loop on the CPU:

#lang java

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        result[i] = f(data[i]);
    }

The same loop in parallel with Java:

#lang java

    IntStream.range(0, n).parallel().forEach(i -> {
        result[i] = f(data[i]);
    });

On the GPU, you *only* *write* *the* *body*. The GPU runs it for every *i*.

: Zeit: 10 Minuten (Block 3, das Modell)
: Das ist das zentrale Bild der ganzen Vorlesung.

* Parallel For in 1, 2 and 3 dimensions

: Ausgabe (Übersicht, kein Code)

    1D:  for i              ⮕ lists, arrays
    2D:  for y, for x       ⮕ images, matrices
    3D:  for z, for y, for x ⮕ volumes, simulations

- You write the body
- The GPU gives you the index: *i*, or *x*, *y*, or *x*, *y*, *z*
- Shadertoy is a 2D Parallel For: one call of your function for every pixel

* There is no outer loop

On the CPU, you prepare things *before* the loop:

#lang go

    table := buildTable()   // once
    for i := range data {
        result[i] = lookup(table, data[i])
    }

On the GPU, there is no "before". You only write the body:

#lang go

    table := buildTable()   // for every element again!
    result[i] = lookup(table, data[i])

- Setup code runs for every element again
- Maybe the compiler optimizes it. Maybe not.

: Pseudocode in Go-Syntax.
: In Shadertoy kommt Setup "von außen" über Uniforms (iTime, iResolution) oder über einen Buffer, der einmal vorberechnet.

* Which loops can you run as a GPU Parallel For?

#lang go

    // A
    for i := range data {
        result[i] = data[i] * 2
    }

    // B
    for i := range data {
        sum += data[i]
    }

    // C
    for i := 1; i < len(data); i++ {
        data[i] = data[i-1] + 1
    }

: Raten lassen, dann weiterklicken.

*

: Leere Folie, damit die Lösung nicht schon am Rand zu sehen ist.

* Which loops can you run as a GPU Parallel For? The answer

- *A:* yes. Every iteration is independent.
- *B:* not directly. All iterations write to the same *sum* (reduction).
- *C:* no. Every iteration needs the result of the previous one.

Three rules of the GPU Parallel For:

- *No* *order:* the iterations run in any order
- *No* *communication:* one iteration cannot wait for the result of another
- *Lockstep:* groups of 32 iterations (a *warp*) execute the same instruction at the same time

: B geht mit einer Reduktion in mehreren Schritten (Baum). Das ist auf der GPU aufwendig.
: Warp ist der NVIDIA-Begriff (32 Threads). AMD spricht von Wavefronts.

* Example: Prime numbers

How do you test whether a number is prime?

: Zeit: 25 Minuten (Block 4, Primzahl-Beispiel)
: TODO Dozent: eigene Folien zum Primzahl-Beispiel (Code, Messungen, Grafiken)
: Gemeinsam mit den Studierenden entwickeln, nicht vorgeben: Probedivision, Abbruch bei sqrt(n), gerade Zahlen.
: Wichtig: Bei einer geraden Zahl ist man sofort fertig, bei einer großen Primzahl läuft die Schleife bis sqrt(n).

* Prime numbers in Java

.code ../src/gpu/primes/Primes.java /START1 OMIT/,/END1 OMIT/

.code ../src/gpu/primes/Primes.java /START2 OMIT/,/END2 OMIT/

- One array, every element is the same prime number: 1,000,003
- A Parallel For over the array: every element is tested on its own
- Deliberately naive: we test all divisors up to n, not only up to √n. So every element has a lot of work.

: Erst nach der gemeinsamen Entwicklung zeigen.
: Ausführbar: cd src/gpu/primes && java Primes.java 10000 (Anzahl der Elemente als Argument, gibt die Laufzeit aus).
: 1 000 003 ist prim (mit BigInteger.isProbablePrime und Probedivision geprüft).
: Der Test ist bewusst naiv (Teiler bis n, nicht bis Wurzel n): Die Schleife läuft etwa 1 000 000 Mal pro Element, und die Arbeit wächst linear mit n. Schlagen die Studierenden die Wurzel vor: richtig, aber für das Experiment brauchen wir viel Arbeit pro Element.
: Alles in 32 Bit, damit derselbe Test auch auf der GPU läuft. Alle Messungen, Grafiken und Tabellen: src/gpu/primes/run.sh (für Apple M3 Max eingestellt)

